\lispPackageChapter{livermore/xcs-set-predicate}

The \texttt{livermore/xcs-set-predicate} package is an allele whose match is
membership in a set, or the whole universe if the covering flag is set.  Share
the \texttt{possible-members} list among predicates of the same problem.

\section{Classes}

\lispClassSubsection{set-predicate}

\begin{description}
\item[\texttt{members}] The values matched when the predicate is not covering.
\item[\texttt{possible-members}] The universe of values this allele may name.
\item[\texttt{covering?}] True if the predicate matches every possible member.
\end{description}

\section{Methods}

\lispFunctionSubsection{non-members}

The possible members that are not in \texttt{members}.  Empty when the
predicate is covering.

\lispFunctionSubsection{universal?}

True if the predicate covers the whole universe, either by the covering flag
or by listing every possible member.

\lispFunctionSubsection{equivalent?}

True if two predicates match the same situations.  A covering predicate and a
predicate that lists every possible member are equivalent.

\texttt{identical?} is stricter: the covering flags must agree as well.

\texttt{match?} against a situation is true if the predicate is covering or
the situation is a member.  \texttt{match?} against another set predicate is
true if this predicate matches everything the other matches.

\texttt{more-general?} is true when this predicate matches the other and they
are not equivalent.

\texttt{cover} builds a predicate whose members are the given situations, and
sets the covering flag with the given probability.

\texttt{mutate} may drop the covering flag, raise it, add a member, or remove
a member other than the current situation.

\texttt{covering-score} is $1$ if covering, otherwise the fraction of possible
members listed.
